How will Lucy Glennon make ends meet now that her housing benefit has been capped?
Freelance writer Lucy Glennon, 26, has made one of the more impressive journeys from Rotherham in South Yorkshire to Kings Cross, Central London, where she settled in 2008 and has since lived what she calls the best years of her life. It's been a life of achievement and independence, led since birth in the constant company of an incurable inherited genetic condition called epidermolysis bullosa – EB for short - whose most explicit symptom is pain all over the body – pain that rarely leaves the sufferer alone.
The briefest bit of research reveals EB as a brute of a disorder, causing the skin to blister and break as a result of almost any kind of friction or bump. Lucy first came to London to do a post-graduate course in magazine journalism, having previously graduated in media and journalism at Sheffield Hallam University. "When the post-grad course finished, I sort of stuck around," she says, though she has never used the capital's crowded Underground or buses – the risk of injury through contact would be too great.
Yet the teeming capital meets many of her most pressing needs. Her fourth floor flat in a mansion block a short distance from Euston Road has two bedrooms, which enables a carer to stay overnight when she's going through a bad patch (Lucy receives two visits a day from a nurse). It is a reasonable taxi ride to St Thomas's hospital, which is an EB centre of excellence. Her father, who still lives in Rotherham, can get to Kings Cross station via Sheffield or Doncaster in 90 minutes or so.
Lucy laughs about the responses her dad got when she first set up home in the capital: "He would say to people, 'She lives near Kings Cross,' and they'd go 'Wooah!'" The more lurid side of the neighbourhood's reputation cuts no ice with her. "It's been really good for me to be here. It's quite a nice community area too. There are lots of shops and it's just really friendly. I just don't want to go. Why would you?"
There is, though, a curt financial answer to her rhetorical question. It is that the government's caps on housing benefit may soon make it impossible for Lucy to afford to pay her rent. At present, this is about £400 a week. Since the start of this month, the amount of housing benefit she receives to help her pay it has been reduced to the new maximum for a two-bedroom home of £290 a week. With the help of advice from a London charity she has successfully applied to her borough, Camden, for a discretionary housing payment of an additional £60 a week, but this will be reviewed at the end of March.
Lucy would love to make a full-time living from journalism – she specialises in disability issues, technology and food - but her ability to do so in a competitive market where freelance opportunities are in diminishing supply is impaired by her illness, which ebbs and flows in intensity, makes her vulnerable to infections and exhausts her. She can walk in short bursts around her rooms, but going out alone is beyond her, especially following a deterioration in her walking capacity four years ago. "I'd probably get tired just going to the ground floor," she says. Much of her basic shopping has to be delivered. Outings are usually taken in the wheelchair she keeps parked by her door. "Luckily, my dad likes walking. He'll go for miles."
So for now Lucy will cover the £50-plus a week gap between her rent and her housing benefit with a portion of her disability living allowance (DLA) and income support. These currently total about £250 a week, and have to go a long way. Although her carer is paid for by Camden council, she has to find between £50 and £70 a month for medical supplies, mostly related to the specialised dressings she needs, that aren't available on prescription.
Lucy has become used to making up the kind of rent shortfall she faces, because until quite recently she'd been receiving £350 a week in housing benefit alone. That sum was the local going rate for flats with just one bedroom, which was all Lucy had been entitled to claim until last April when the government introduced an exemption for disabled people who sometimes needed a carer to stay overnight. That meant she could claim for her second bedroom too.
