[caption id="attachment_98130" align="alignnone" width="580"] My Morning Jacket frontman Jim James live in Brooklyn last month. Photo: Theo Wargo, Getty Images.[/caption]
My Morning Jacket has been called a Southern Rock band since it began in Louisville a decade and a half ago, but I've always thought that simplistic tag was unfair: Take away their flannel shirts, their mess of long hair and singer Jim James' occasional drawl and they have just as much in common with Pink Floyd as they do Lynyrd Skynyrd, their reverb-drenched wails howling not just with grit, but druggy psychedelia as well.
That said, for their first of three nights at the Wiltern this week, an unrestrained MMJ unleashed on a depressingly 2/3-full room (they deserve it packed and sweaty rather than pleasant and roomy) in a way the group hasn't done locally in a long while.
The last time the band was in town, the focus was on last year's mostly mellow sixth album Circuital, which meant set lists were often full of meandering melodies rather than full-boar explosiveness. This tour, however, is a somewhat fan-curated celebration of the band's entire catalog; this L.A. run, if it follows suit with previous tour stops, will include no repeated songs by the time it's over Thursday night.
That has set the stage for an insane level of unpredictability, noticeable in Tuesday night's rarer selections alongside fan favorites like "I'm Amazed" and "Gideon." There was the old-old-old-school track "Old September Blues," for instance, no longer a swamp-driven porch song but instead an exploration of intricate finger-picking. Plus there were well-suited but sometimes out-there covers, like the Band's "It Makes No Difference," Prince's "I Could Never Take the Place of Your Man" and, the standout, "Rocket Man" -- yes, the Elton John classic, with James plaintively plucking his acoustic guitar.
It made for a night constructed for die-hards yet still satisfying for casual fans, full of freak-out guitar-rocking, cape-wearing dramatics and a laser-ific light show that could put anyone in a daze, drug-induced or not. Allowing themselves room to let loose has always been how My Morning Jacket shines the most. Without a new album to promote, they're clearly in that zone -- and that's making things gorgeously unclear at shows like this one.
