Timothy Garton Ash is right to say that a sustainable economy "requires us to change as well as our states" (This epochal crisis requires us to resolve the paradox of capitalism, 7 May). But his formulation of the challenge is less radical than it could be. Garton Ash's analysis hinges on the sociologist Daniel Bell: "He suggested that the production side depends on people harking to values such as hard work, punctuality, discipline and a readiness to accept deferred gratification. The demand side, by contrast, depends on them being self-indulgent, expansive, pleasure-seeking and given to living in the now."
But this contrast is not a tension; rather, it is complementary - discipline and self-discipline at work, sold as the price for hedonism outside work. It's hard to rebalance this basic bargain. Consuming more responsibly can do some good (such as fair trade), but as an ecological measure it is vulnerable to rebound effects: I save money or carbon in one part of my life and feel entitled to spend more in another. More efficient refrigerators can lead people to buy bigger ones. Meanwhile, the fragmentation of production makes it hard to rebuild broad social solidarity there, despite the unions' best efforts. The task then is not so much to "find a new balance" between these already interlocked roles, but to question the roles themselves.
"Producer" and "consumer" both reside in what Hegel called "civil society", the realm of the economy and its regulation. As republicanism has long proclaimed, this omits the domain of collective responsibility and solidarity: the domain of citizenship. But recurrent calls to revitalise citizenship often fall into the same trap as Garton Ash, merely bolting a third identity on to his two. That makes us producers in our day jobs, consumers on the weekends. And citizens - when? As Oscar Wilde said of socialism, the trouble with citizenship is that it takes up too many evenings - when our consumer-selves would rather be at the pub or the shopping mall.
